From: Sobs <antohasobne@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] video: bareapp: OpenGL pictures copied by the GPU, read a
 frame later

SDL_BAREAPP_GL_ASYNC=1 (a game's game.ini [env]): at each swap the GPU
copies the picture into one of two renderbuffers, scaled to the size it
gets in bare_app's frame, with a fence; the copy made at the swap before
(finished by then) is read back and shown. The game's work on the next
frame overlaps the GPU's on this one instead of waiting for it in
glReadPixels, and a scaled picture costs no CPU; the picture is a frame
late. When frames come more than 100 ms apart (a game loading), each is
shown at its own swap, as the next one may be long in coming. Mesa's
panfrost reads pixel buffer objects back on the CPU too, so those would
not help. Desktop OpenGL reads GL_BGRA, bare_app's own layout: a plain
copy. Needs OpenGL (ES) 3 by its version string (eglGetProcAddress has
something for every name) and no multisampling (a scaling blit cannot
resolve it); else, and by default, pictures are read at each swap.

Both ways the window is read whatever the game has bound: draw and read
framebuffers apart and a pixel pack buffer (OpenGL (ES) 3) too. The
copies belong to the context they were made in: when it is deleted (SDL
has made it no longer current) or another one swaps, they are forgotten,
not deleted in a context they are not part of.
